    ],
    "word_count": 50,
    "html": "<p>Paul Lincoln Heffner was an American investment manager based in Hong Kong who co-founded and led Adamas Asset Management. He previously built Ajia Partners’ fund-of-funds business and worked in private wealth management at Morgan Stanley. A Columbia MBA graduate, he combined investment responsibilities with ownership of his own asset-management firm.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Paul Heffner first went to China as a student in 1986, studying at Nanjing University before settling full-time in Hong Kong in 1994. As an investment manager, he worked with growth businesses seeking finance beyond conventional bank lending. His firm partnered with Ping An Trust to provide secured funding in Greater China.\n\nHeffner argued that investment decisions should consider a business’s wider effects alongside its financial prospects. At Adamas, he described assessing proposals against environmental, social and governance criteria, including education, gender equality and responsible production. He regarded investing as a partnership and looked at the opportunities technology could create for businesses.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 87,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "First-person roundtable and complete indexed KPMG interview support the formation and stated professional approach. These are his described criteria, not independently certified outcomes."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S3",
      "S4"
    ],
    "word_count": 102,
    "html": "<p>Paul Heffner first went to China as a student in 1986, studying at Nanjing University before settling full-time in Hong Kong in 1994. As an investment manager, he worked with growth businesses seeking finance beyond conventional bank lending. His firm partnered with Ping An Trust to provide secured funding in Greater China.</p>\r\n<p>Heffner argued that investment decisions should consider a business’s wider effects alongside its financial prospects. At Adamas, he described assessing proposals against environmental, social and governance criteria, including education, gender equality and responsible production. He regarded investing as a partnership and looked at the opportunities technology could create for businesses.</p>\r\n"
  },
